{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>I did not castrate them.</b> It is the way of the world that a shepherd strikes the flock and drives them hard, and thereby they become castrated. But I did not castrate them; rather, I led them slowly." "<b>And the rams of your flock.</b> Rashi interpreted from here that a one-day-old is called an איל (ram). And if you ask: but we say in general that it is not called an איל before two years, and one who is obligated to bring an איל אשם (ram of guilt-offering) must bring a two-year-old ram — one may answer that certainly a one-day-old is called an איל, but for an offering it must be two years old. <b>And the rams of your flock I did not eat:</b> The custom of a shepherd — when he drives the flock to a distant place to find pasture of grass and does not find it, to buy himself food, because he has gone far from settlement — he takes from the rams of the flock that are not fit for breeding and eats them. But as for me — <b>and the rams of your flock I did not eat.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:57.716339804Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}